<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>The curriculum is the sales page</h2>
		<p>Every lesson title is visible before anybody pays — <strong>including the locked ones</strong>. A course that hides its contents is telling a buyer it is not sure they are worth the money, and the titles are the best argument it has.</p>
		<p>The <a href="/navigation/curriculum/">curriculum component</a> does all of it: modules that open, lessons inside, and four states on each one — <code>done</code> (a filled tick), <code>aria-current="page"</code> (the accent, "you are here"), <code>locked</code> (a padlock, still readable), and nothing at all for one not started.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Built from</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>The head</td><td><code>im-projecthero</code> — the same one a <a href="/collections/project/page/">project</a> uses.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The numbers</td><td><code>im-projectstats</code>: lessons, length, modules, and what you keep.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The curriculum</td><td><code>im-curriculum</code> with <code>position: static</code> — on the course page it is content, not a rail.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The buy box</td><td><code>im-buybox</code> from the <a href="/collections/shop/product/">product page</a>, with a <a href="/components/progress/">progress bar</a> instead of a licence chooser once somebody has started.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p><strong>The button says what it does next.</strong> "Continue — lesson 3" for somebody enrolled, "Buy" for somebody not. One button that says "Start course" to both is a button that lies to one of them.</p>
	</div>
</section>
